.\" hydra-parity(1)
.TH HYDRA\-PARITY 1 "2026-08-17" "hydra" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
hydra-parity \- local Reed-Solomon parity for an at-rest file
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hydra parity make
.BI \-\-manifest " FILE"
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.br
.B hydra parity repair
.BI \-\-manifest " FILE"
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
For the
.B at-rest
case only: a file whose source is gone and whose bytes may have rotted.
.PP
While the source is still reachable, do not use this.
A targeted refetch driven by
.B "hydra \-\-chunk\-digests"
is roughly 400x cheaper, and coding buys no download speed at all \(em on a
mirror set that honours
.BR Range ,
measured makespan is identical with and without parity.
Parity is generated
.I after
a download has verified, never fetched during one.
.
.SS Digests first, parity second
The order is not a preference.
Reed-Solomon erasure decoding handed an
.I unflagged
corrupt shard reported success in 100% of 200 trials per corruption size
while producing correct bytes in 0% \(em a single flipped byte sufficed.
Parity without digests therefore makes a client
.I less
trustworthy than no parity at all: a whole-object hash mismatch at least
says something is wrong, whereas an unflagged RS "repair" returns corrupt
data with a success code.
.PP
So the manifest is the prerequisite and supplies the erasure positions.
Without them there is no repair, because a decoder cannot tell a corrupt
shard from a good one.
There is no mode that guesses them, and positions are only accepted from a
manifest read off a
.I local path
\(em the trusted level in
.IR docs/INTEGRITY.md " \(sc1."
.
.SH make
Generate parity for
.I FILE
alongside its manifest, and record the code
.I in
that manifest \(em including the parity shards' own digests, since a corrupt
parity shard fed into a repair produces a silently wrong result.
.PP
Shard size equals the manifest's chunk size.
A download chunk straddling two shards would mean one corrupt chunk destroys
two shards, halving effective parity.
.TP
.I FILE
The file to protect.
.TP
.BI \-\-manifest " FILE"
Manifest describing the file's chunk grid, as written by
.BR "hydra \-\-emit\-manifest" .
Required.
It is updated in place.
.TP
.BI \-\-parity " N"
Number of parity shards.
Repair succeeds if and only if damaged shards
.RI <= " N" .
Default 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
Where to write the parity.
Defaults to
.IB FILE .parity .
Prefer a
.B separate device:
drive checksum errors show strong spatial locality, so parity stored beside
its data can die with it.
.TP
.BI \-\-window " BYTES"
Repair window.
Memory is O(n * window), not O(file), so resident memory is independent of
file size.
Default 65536.
.PP
On success the command reports the shard geometry, the window, the
destination, how many damaged shards it can repair, and the resident memory
the repair will need.
.
.SH repair
Repair a damaged file using its manifest and parity.
The file is scanned chunk by chunk against the manifest; mismatching chunks
become the erasure positions.
.TP
.I FILE
The damaged file.
.TP
.BI \-\-manifest " FILE"
Manifest recording the chunk grid and, from
.BR make ,
the parity metadata.
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-parity\-file " FILE"
Parity to use.
Defaults to what the manifest recorded.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be repaired without writing.
.PP
If every chunk verifies, the command says so and exits 0 without touching
the file.
If more chunks are damaged than there are parity shards, it names both
counts and refuses rather than attempting a decode that cannot succeed.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Parity generated, repair completed, or nothing needed repairing.
.TP
.B 1
Generation or repair failed, or the damage exceeds the parity available.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: the manifest could not be read or parsed, the geometry is
invalid, or the manifest records no parity for a
.B repair
run.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB FILE .parity
Default parity output.
.TP
.I manifest
Rewritten by
.B make
to add a
.B parity
block:
.BR algo ,
.B field
.RB ( gf16 ),
.BR k ,
.BR parity_shards ,
.BR window ,
.BR file ,
and per-shard digests.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Protect an archived file whose source is gone:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra \-\-emit\-manifest cold.json \-O cold.bin https://example.com/cold.bin
hydra parity make cold.bin \-\-manifest cold.json \e
      \-\-parity 3 \-\-out /other\-disk/cold.parity
.fi
.RE
.PP
Years later, check and repair:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra parity repair cold.bin \-\-manifest cold.json \-\-dry\-run
hydra parity repair cold.bin \-\-manifest cold.json
.fi
.RE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hydra (1),
.BR hydra\-checksum (1)
.PP
.IR docs/INTEGRITY.md .
